---
import CaseStudy from "../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro";
import Figure from "../../components/Figure.astro";

const metrics = [
  { value: "68.5%", label: "Regression R², N = 2,295" },
  { value: "2 of 5", label: "Markdowns with measurable return" },
  { value: "5x", label: "Store size against any promotional lever" },
  { value: "$48,799", label: "Weekly lift per store" },
];

const links = [
  { label: "Deck (PDF)", href: "/media/Linh_Le_Walmart_Promotional_Strategy.pdf" },
];
---

<CaseStudy
  title="Only two of five markdown types paid for themselves"
  description="Weekly sales across Walmart store formats, with three of five promotional levers returning nothing and store size outweighing every lever a manager can actually pull. Case study by Linh (Min) Le."
  project="Walmart Promotional Strategy"
  period="April 2026"
  tint="viz"
  hook="A one percent lift in chain-wide weekly revenue is worth one to two billion dollars a year, which is why it matters that most of the promotional budget was buying nothing."
  metrics={metrics}
  links={links}
>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 class="h-sub">The decision this informed</h2>
        <p>
          Promotional spend is one of the few levers a retail management team can move inside a single
          budget cycle, and it is spent across store formats that behave nothing alike. The question was
          how to adjust promotional and inventory strategy across those formats to improve weekly sales,
          which in practice means deciding what to stop funding.
        </p>
        <p>
          I led the diagnostic work on a team of four, taking the analysis from raw weekly sales
          records through to the reallocation recommendation.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 class="h-sub">The finding</h2>
        <p>
          Holding store size, store type and holiday timing constant, only two of the five markdown
          categories produced a measurable return. MarkDown3 returns <strong>$10.12</strong> in weekly
          store sales for every dollar spent, and MarkDown5 returns <strong>$5.25</strong>. The
          regression carries an <strong>R&sup2; of 68.5%</strong> across <strong>2,295</strong>
          store-week observations. The other three returned nothing statistically distinguishable from
          zero.
        </p>
        <p>
          The effect is not uniform across formats, which is where the operational instruction lives.
          Run separately, the Type C model explains <strong>2.3%</strong> of sales variation and fails
          its F-test at p = 0.330, so markdowns account for essentially nothing in that format. Running
          the return calculation store by store inside it surfaced a single location, Store 30, at an
          ROI ratio of <strong>&minus;1.60</strong> on roughly $2,534 of weekly markdown spend. The
          other five Type C stores all showed positive expected lift, which is why the recommendation
          cuts one store instead of writing off the segment.
        </p>
        <p>
          The holiday effect, which is the assumption every retail calendar is built on, holds only in
          the larger formats. Type A and B stores show a statistically significant seasonal lift. In
          Type C the lift is negative and lands at p = 0.914, indistinguishable from noise, so seasonal
          inventory and staffing built on a chain-wide holiday assumption is misallocated by
          construction. Once the macroeconomic variables enter the full model, the holiday flag drops
          out entirely.
        </p>
        <p>
          Sitting above all of it is a variable nobody can act on. Store size carries a standardised
          coefficient of <strong>0.848</strong>, more than <strong>five times</strong> the weight of any
          promotional factor, against 0.152 for MarkDown3 and 0.055 for MarkDown5. It sets the ceiling
          and cannot be changed inside a budget cycle, which is precisely why the levers underneath it
          have to be allocated well. Two recommendations together project a <strong>$48,799</strong>
          weekly increase per store, a <strong>4.66%</strong> lift against the $1,046,965 average
          weekly baseline: moving a fifth of the budget out of the three dead markdown types into the
          two that work in Type A and B stores, and cutting Store 30's markdown spend to redirect it
          where the return is confirmed.
        </p>
        <Figure
          src="/figures/walmart-revenue-scenarios.png"
          alt="Bar chart of weekly revenue by recommendation scenario. Baseline 1,046,965 dollars, Scenario A adding 23,153, Scenario B adding 25,646, for a grand total of 1,095,764 dollars."
          caption="The two recommendations against the $1,046,965 weekly baseline. Moving a fifth of the markdown budget into the two working types adds $23,153. Cutting Store 30 and redirecting that spend adds $25,646. Together, $48,799 a week per store."
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 class="h-sub">What I did, and the obvious alternative I rejected</h2>
        <p>
          Two rejections shaped the analysis, and the second one is the one I would defend in an
          interview.
        </p>
        <p>
          The first was ranking stores on sales volume, which is the default view in every retail
          dashboard and which conflates two different things. Growth rate and sales volume answer
          different questions, and the store with the strongest and most stable growth in the entire
          chain averages about <strong>$386,000</strong> a week, under forty percent of what a typical
          store generates. Reading that store as a success or a failure depends entirely on which
          column you sorted by, so the analysis kept them separate and plotted growth against
          volatility rather than collapsing them.
        </p>
        <Figure
          src="/figures/walmart-growth-vs-volatility.png"
          alt="Scatter plot of every store by growth rate on the horizontal axis and weekly sales volatility on the vertical axis, with the strongest and weakest performers annotated."
          caption="Growth against volatility, every store plotted. Store 38 sits far right and low, the strongest and steadiest grower in the chain. A ranking by total sales would have buried it, because it is a small-format store."
          wide
        />
        <div class="pull">
          The second was treating one model as the answer. The same analysis was run again as a
          store-level fixed-effects model, directed through an AI coding agent, replacing three store
          type dummies with forty-four individual store dummies so each location learns its own
          baseline. Explanatory power rose sharply, from <strong>68.5% to 93.1%</strong>, which says
          most of what the first model left unexplained was store-specific behaviour that type-level
          controls could not capture.
        </div>
        <p>
          What did not move is the finding. Both specifications agree that MarkDown3 and MarkDown5 are
          the significant levers, and the coefficient signs hold across every predictor. That is the
          result worth having: two methods with different assumptions about what drives store-level
          variation, landing on the same instruction. A richer model that had reversed the ranking would
          have meant the first answer was an artefact of how stores were grouped.
        </p>
        <p>
          Forecast validity was tested by holding out fourteen periods rather than by reporting fit on
          the data the model had already seen. Aggregate holdout error came in at
          <strong>&minus;0.352%</strong>, and testing stores chosen for contrasting growth and
          volatility profiles produced errors from &minus;0.0115% to &minus;1.644%. A store's growth
          pattern turns out to have little bearing on how well its sales can be forecast, which means
          the reallocation projection holds across the chain rather than only in the stable stores.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <header class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Evidence</p>
        <h2 class="h-sub">The deck as presented</h2>
        <p class="section-note">
          Nineteen slides moving from descriptive through diagnostic and predictive to the
          reallocation recommendation.
        </p>
      </header>
      <div class="embed">
        <iframe src="/media/Linh_Le_Walmart_Promotional_Strategy.pdf"
                title="Walmart promotional strategy deck" loading="lazy"></iframe>
      </div>
      <p class="embed-note">
        <a href="/media/Linh_Le_Walmart_Promotional_Strategy.pdf">Open the deck in its own tab</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 class="h-sub">Assumptions and limitations</h2>
        <p>
          The markdown categories arrive anonymised in the source data, identified by number rather
          than by what they actually are. The analysis can say which two work and cannot say what they
          are, which limits the recommendation to a reallocation instruction and stops it short of a
          merchandising explanation.
        </p>
        <p>
          The reallocation figure assumes spend moves into the working categories at the returns those
          categories have already demonstrated. That holds while the shift is marginal and becomes
          unreliable at larger volumes, where diminishing returns would bite and the historical
          coefficients would stop describing the new spend level.
        </p>
        <p>
          The $48,799 is per store per week under those conditions. It is a projection from
          observational data rather than a measured outcome, and the honest test would be a controlled
          reallocation in a subset of stores before committing the chain.
        </p>
        <p>
          The negative-return finding rests on a single store within a format that shows no markdown
          response overall, so it is a flag for investigation rather than a conclusion about that
          location.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</CaseStudy>
